        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Canals, the beach and the neighborhoods in between</strong></p><p>Fort Lauderdale earned the Venice of America name from its canals. Las Olas Isles, Rio Vista, Harbor Beach and Coral Ridge are finger-island streets. The lots end at a seawall, often with a dock, and the pool deck sits in between. Those decks are where a frameless glass railing earns its keep: it closes off the pool without hiding the water. Along A1A, from Fort Lauderdale Beach to the Galt Ocean Mile, condo towers face the Atlantic with concrete balconies on every floor.</p><p>Victoria Park and Flagler Village add townhomes with rooftop decks near downtown and the New River. Wilton Manors sits on the Middle River. Lauderdale-by-the-Sea and Pompano Beach continue the beach to the north, Hollywood and Dania Beach to the south. Plantation, Davie and Weston are the inland suburbs, with pool homes and far less salt in the air.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Broward County is in the HVHZ</strong></p><p>Broward County, with Miami-Dade, makes up the High-Velocity Hurricane Zone of the Florida Building Code, 8th edition (2023). The HVHZ carries stricter wind-load rules than the rest of Florida, and Broward's building departments review railing attachments. Our <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-engineering-testing\">engineering documents</a> give the testing and blocking details for top-mount and side-mount spigots on concrete and on wood. Fort Lauderdale, Pompano Beach, Hollywood and Wilton Manors each run their own permit office under the county's Board of Rules and Appeals.</p><p>Heights follow the state code. A house deck or balcony more than 30 inches above grade needs 36 inches; condos and other multifamily buildings need 42. A pool barrier under the Residential Swimming Pool Safety Act is at least 48 inches on the outside. Gates close on their own and open away from the pool. See our <a href=\"/pages/florida-building-code-for-glass-railings\">Florida building code page</a>, then confirm with your building department.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Delivery and installation in Fort Lauderdale</strong></p><p>Standard orders are processed in 2 to 3 business days and arrive in 5 to 10 business days by freight carrier with a liftgate. On the isles, the truck lowers the crate to the driveway. For a Galt Ocean Mile or beachfront tower, book the loading dock and freight elevator with management and tell us the address type. Custom sizes take 2 to 3 weeks in transit after processing. Orders over 3,000 dollars ship free, and the checkout price is final: no duties, tariffs or hidden fees.</p><p>Installation is DIY with the installation guide and videos, and a standard railing takes about 4 hours. Each panel sits in two spigots, fixed to the concrete pool deck, the balcony slab or wood framing, with screws sold separately. Check the crate within 3 days of delivery; damage reported before installation is replaced or refunded at no cost.</p>"
          }
        }
